Kangal are large, independent, and powerful livestock guardians bred to assess threats and protect territory on their own. Training them requires a firm, patient, and positive approach rather than harsh force or intimidation.


Core Training Principles

Early Socialization: Expose puppies to various people, animals, and  enviroments between 8 and 16 weeks old to prevent excessive suspision or fear-based aggression.

Positive Reinforcement: Use treats, praise, and consistency to motivate obedience, keeping short training sessions around 10 to 15 minutes due to their independent nature.

Obedience & Recall: Teach reliable basic commands(sit, stay, come, heel) early so you maintain control over a massive, strong-willed dog.

Bite Inhibition: Curb rough mouthing immediately during puppyhood by stopping play when they bite too hard.

Provide a Job: Kangals thrive when given a clear purpose-such as property and livestock survillance and at least 2 hours of daily physical and mental exercise (like puzzle toys or space to run).

